NAME
    vi - modal screen editor

SYNOPSIS
    vi [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
    A vi-style modal editor. Loads FILE into the buffer, draws the
    screen via direct sys_tty_cursor positioning, and reads keys in
    TTY_RAW mode.

MODES
    Normal mode (default on start)
    Insert mode (entered via i / I / a / A / o / O)
    Command-line mode (entered via :)

KEYS (normal mode)
    h j k l         cursor left / down / up / right
    w / b           jump word forward / backward
    0 / $           start / end of line
    gg / G          first / last line of file
    i / a           enter insert mode (at cursor / after cursor)
    I / A           enter insert mode (start / end of current line)
    o / O           open line below / above + enter insert mode
    x               delete char under cursor
    dd              delete current line
    yy              yank current line to register
    p               paste register after cursor
    u               undo last change (limited; sessions 46+ heritage)
    :               enter command-line mode

COMMAND-LINE MODE (:)
    :w [FILE]       write to FILE (default: loaded file)
    :q              quit (refuses if unsaved)
    :q!             quit unconditionally
    :wq             write then quit
    :N              jump to line N
    :/pat           search forward for pat

EXAMPLES
    vi hello.txt
    :w   :q

EXIT STATUS
    0   clean quit
    1   command syntax error or write failure

SEE ALSO
    ed(1), sh(1)

NOTES
    Cursor positioning uses SYS_TTY_CURSOR, which updates the
    framebuffer and VGA grid but not the host terminal's idea
    of cursor over -serial stdio. The QEMU graphical window
    renders vi correctly; the serial side gets a redraw-only
    view. Limitation acknowledged; ANSI-CSI emission in fbcon
    would fix both at once but is a separate session.
